    "submission": "submission_c15.json",
    "summary": "Fixed direct check (action=check) for unit-claim-timeout-now-attribution, preflight-validated with valid=True and no diagnostics.\n\nDesign: plan_c3.json plus harness_tn_test.go build Raft nodes with all background goroutines disabled and a bootstrapped two-voter configuration, give each a known leader (via the same setLeader/setState accessors appendEntries uses) and drive the production dispatch seam processRPC with constructed TimeoutNow requests — an unattributed sender absent from the configuration, a configured-voter control, and an out-of-window control. Declarative prerequisite: sender_is_configured_voter false with configuration_nonempty true. Monitor applicability: rpc_header_in_window true. Property: an implication — if the sender is not a configured voter then the transfer privilege must not be granted — with identity op_id, evaluated from the emitted event, not tests_passed.\n\nConstruction evidence (not the formal observation): running the harness locally in a copy of the captured module shows the unattributed sender was honoured exactly like the configured sender (leader node2 cleared, Follower to Candidate, privilege false to true, rejected false), while the out-of-window request was rejected with 'operation not supported with current protocol version' and changed nothing — so the only guard on this path is the version window. gofmt and go vet are clean against the actual package.\n\nDeclared limitations: nodes use skipStartup so no main loop runs; the sender identifier is fabricated rather than a real removed running node (the check establishes the handler's decision for a sender absent from the receiver's configuration, not that such a sender reaches it in a deployment); and the downstream consequence for a healthy leader is not measured. The plan carries the deliberate-design alternative (if only a genuine leader ever sends TimeoutNow, the correct disposition is a documented assumption) as legality alternatives and as an uncertainty. No verdict is claimed; fresh execution of these fixed bytes plus a checker-correspondence review still remain."
